course: dictionary
lesson: 5364
title: One word, many meanings: kiss
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Kiss can be a verb (an action) or a noun (a thing). Context tells you which. In «The King says the Cat may kiss his hand», kiss is something you do: to touch someone with your lips, as a greeting.
explain: Look at the words around the word. «The ball just kisses the post» does not mean the ball kissed anyone. The clue is just: the ball touches the post very lightly, and it does not hit it hard. So kiss can also mean a very light touch.
explain: Words can also tell you about feelings and safety. «People have a right to say no to hugs, kisses or tickling, even from family.» This shows that you are allowed to refuse a kiss, even from someone you know, and others should respect your choice.
fact: Kiss can be a verb (to kiss) or a noun (a kiss, kisses).
fact: To kiss means to touch someone with your lips to show love, care or a greeting.
fact: Kiss can mean a very light touch, as when a ball just kisses the post.
fact: At the end of a card or message, the letter x can mean a kiss, which means love.
fact: Your body belongs to you, and you can always say no to a kiss, even from a relative.
exercise: «The King says the Cat may kiss his hand.» What does kiss mean here? | To chase someone away | To sing a happy song | *To touch someone with the lips as a greeting | To bite something hard
exercise: «The ball just kissed the post and rolled away.» What does kiss mean in this sentence? | *To touch very lightly | To hit very hard and break | To shout loudly | To lift high in the air
exercise: A card ends with «Love from Mum, xx». What do the x letters mean? | A wrong spelling | A number of days | *A kiss, which shows love | The name of a town
test: «You can say no to a kiss, even from a relative.» What does this tell you? | Relatives can kiss anyone they like | You must always hug your family | Kisses are only for grown-ups | *Your body belongs to you, and you can refuse
test: «Blow a kiss» is a phrase. What is a kiss in this phrase? | *The touch of lips, sent through the air as a greeting or sign of love | A kind of fish | A loud noise
exam: «Kissing a frog will not make a prince, and many toads have poison behind their eyes.» Why is this a warning? | Frogs are princes in disguise | Toads only kiss in fairy tales | *Toads can be poisonous, so kissing one can make you ill | Frogs are always too big to hold
